(Abstract) 41 72 625 DS
% Font[Serif, 0, 10]
343 10 4 SF
(Combining scripting languages such as Tcl with lower-level programming languages such as Java offers new ) 443 72 607 DS
(opportunities for flexible and rapid software development. In this paper, we benchmark various combinations of Tcl ) 468 72 595 DS
(and Java against the two languages alone. We also provide some comparisons with JavaScript. Performance can ) 451 72 584 DS
(vary by well over two orders of magnitude. We also uncovered some interesting threading issues that affect ) 433 72 572 DS
(performance on the Solaris platform.) 147 72 560 DS
("There are lies, damn lies and statistics") 161 72 540 DS
(This paper is a work in progress, we used the information here to give our group some generalizations on the ) 441 72 519 DS
(performance tradeoffs between various scripting languages. Updating the timing results to include JDK1.2 with a ) 457 72 508 DS
(Just In Time \(JIT\) compiler would be useful.) 181 72 496 DS
% Font[Serif, 1, 11]
392 11 5 SF
(Introduction) 59 72 476 DS
% Font[Serif, 0, 10]
343 10 4 SF
(There is a growing trend towards integration of multiple languages through ) 305 72 450 DS
% Font[Serif, 2, 10]
331 10 6 SF
(scripting) 36 377 450 DS
% Font[Serif, 0, 10]
343 10 4 SF
(. In a famously controversial ) 118 413 450 DS
(white paper ) 49 72 438 DS
32 32 192 SC
(\(Ousterhout 97\)) 63 121 438 DS
121 437 184 437 DL
0 0 0 SC
(, John Ousterhout, now of Scriptics Corporation, argues that scripting -- the use of a ) 349 184 438 DS
(high-level, untyped, interpreted language to "glue" together components written in a lower-level language -- ) 447 72 426 DS
(provides greater reuse benefits that other reuse technologies. Although traditionally a language such as C or C++ ) 456 72 415 DS
(has been the lower-level language, more recent efforts have focused on using Java. Recently, Sun Microsystems ) 454 72 403 DS
(laboratories announced two products aimed at fulfilling this goal with the Tcl and Java programming languages. ) 454 72 391 DS
(The goals of these products are to "create a synergy between Java and Tcl" ) 300 72 380 DS
32 32 192 SC
(\(Johnson 98\)) 52 372 380 DS
372 379 424 379 DL
0 0 0 SC
(.) 3 424 380 DS
(The first product, Tcl Blend, is a C interface between the Tcl interpreter and the Java virtual machine \(JVM\). The ) 458 72 359 DS
(Tcl programmer is provided with a few commands that create and manipulate Java objects. Tcl Blend originated in ) 464 72 347 DS
(a simpler interface named TclJava which was created by Scott Stanton and Ken Corey ) 347 72 336 DS
32 32 192 SC
(\(Stanton and Corey 96\)) 94 419 336 DS
419 335 513 335 DL
0 0 0 SC
( of ) 14 513 336 DS
(Sun Laboratories. The second, Jacl ) 143 72 324 DS
32 32 192 SC
(\(Lam 97\)) 37 215 324 DS
215 323 252 323 DL
0 0 0 SC
(, is an implementation of the Tcl interpreter written entirely in Java. ) 277 252 324 DS
(This project originated as a research project developed by Ioi Lam and Brian Smith of Cornell University.) 426 72 312 DS
