(ack) 15 72 376 DS
% Font[Serif, 0, 10]
343 10 4 SF
(This tests compute Ackermann's function, which is an excellent test for recursive function calls. According to ) 444 72 358 DS
(Kernighan and Van Wyk, ack\(3,k\) requires at least 4^\(k+1\) function calls and reaches a recursive depth of ) 426 72 347 DS
(2^\(k+3\)-1.) 46 72 335 DS
(Here is the Tcl implementation of the Ackermann function:) 238 72 315 DS
% Font[Monospaced, 0, 10]
378 10 8 SF
(  proc ack {m n} {) 108 72 298 DS
() 0 180 298 DS
(      if {$m == 0} { return [expr $n+1] }) 246 72 287 DS
() 0 318 287 DS
(      if {$n == 0} { return [ack [expr $m-1] 1] }) 294 72 275 DS
() 0 366 275 DS
(      return [ack [expr $m-1] [ack $m [expr $n-1]]]) 306 72 264 DS
() 0 378 264 DS
(  }) 18 72 253 DS
% Font[Serif, 0, 10]
343 10 4 SF
(We attempted to run this test for ack\(3,5\), ack\(3,6\), ack\(3,7\), ack\(3,8\). Tcl7.6, Tcl8.0 and Jacl ran out of stack ) 447 72 236 DS
(space for ack\(3,6\) and greater. \(Kernighan and Van Wyk note this problem in their paper.\) The JavaScript version, ) 460 72 224 DS
(running in Netscape4.04 under Solaris, causes Netscape to segfault while computing ack\(3,5\). The obvious ) 433 72 212 DS
(conclusion is to not use these languages for intensely-recursive functions.) 298 72 201 DS
