343 10 4 SF
(This test performs various operations on a string, such as getting a range of characters, taking the length, and ) 441 72 442 DS
(concatenating strings. Kernighan and Van Wyk's Tcl implementation looks like this:) 342 72 430 DS
% Font[Monospaced, 0, 10]
378 10 8 SF
(  for {set j 0} {$j  10} {incr j} {) 210 72 413 DS
() 0 282 413 DS
(      set s "abcdef") 120 72 402 DS
() 0 192 402 DS
(      while {[string length $s] = $n} {) 234 72 391 DS
() 0 306 391 DS
(          set s "123${s}456${s}789") 210 72 380 DS
() 0 282 380 DS
(          set s "[string range $s [expr [string length $s]/2] end] \\) 408 72 369 DS
() 0 480 369 DS
(                 [string range $s 0 [expr [string length $s]/2]]") 390 72 358 DS
() 0 462 358 DS
(      }) 42 72 347 DS
() 0 114 347 DS
(  }) 18 72 336 DS
% Font[Serif, 0, 10]
343 10 4 SF
(We ran this test with ) 87 72 309 DS
% Font[Serif, 2, 10]
331 10 6 SF
(n) 5 159 309 DS
% Font[Serif, 0, 10]
343 10 4 SF
( equal to 62500, 125000, 250000 and 500000. The JavaScript version of the test consumed ) 371 164 309 DS
(all system memory \(Netscape 4.04 under Solaris\).) 201 72 298 DS
(As noted by Kernighan and Van Wyk, the performance of the memory allocators plays a large factor in this test. ) 453 72 277 DS
(Notable is the fact that the byte-compiled Tcl code is consistently several times faster than Java, showing the ) 444 72 265 DS
(advantage of languages with C-code native functions. Another interesting point was that even Jacl was sometimes ) 463 72 254 DS
(faster than the fully-interpreted Tcl version. The JavaScript data is all over the place, because JavaScript was ) 440 72 242 DS
(thrashing the system's memory allocator.) 166 72 230 DS
