(The input/output tests produced a number of surprising results. First, the byte-coded Tcl interpreter often ) 428 72 440 DS
(performed worse then the naive \(7.6\) Tcl interpreter. In addition, both of these were often faster than Java, ) 428 72 428 DS
(presumably because their I/O is hand-coded in C, whereas the Java I/O is coded in Java \(and quite complex as ) 447 72 416 DS
(well\).) 23 72 405 DS
(A third result that we were not at all prepared for is the performance difference between Tcl Blend and Java. In most ) 468 72 384 DS
(of the tests, this comes in at about a factor of two. We were mystified by this at first -- surely it should be the ) 452 72 373 DS
(same, as for all the other tests? After some experimentation \(read "desperate hacking"\), we discovered that the ) 442 72 361 DS
(performance difference is due almost entirely to the threading package used by the Java virtual machine: the native ) 461 72 349 DS
(threads package on Solaris cause I/O to run at half the speed achieved with the Green threads package.) 408 72 337 DS
(We confirmed this fact with the ) 130 72 317 DS
% Font[Monospaced, 0, 9]
315 9 8 SF
(tail) 20 202 317 DS
% Font[Serif, 0, 10]
343 10 4 SF
( test for several different combinations. In the following, the JNI test is a ) 297 222 317 DS
(simple C program that calls Java directly through the Java Native Interface:) 302 72 305 DS
