72 720 468 -648 RC
% Font[Serif, 1, 11]
392 11 5 SF
(Discussion and Conclusions) 126 72 457 DS
% Font[Serif, 0, 10]
343 10 4 SF
(The tests contained a lot of results that we expected, either from the tests performed by Kernighan and Van Wyk, or ) 468 72 431 DS
(from our own intuitions about the relative performance the various implementations. They also contained many we ) 465 72 419 DS
(didn't expect!) 55 72 408 DS
(For numeric and control processing, Java is fastest. However, string processing and file I/O is better done in Tcl. ) 457 72 387 DS
(The use of Tcl Blend to call Java had no measurable effect in performance -- except for the factor-of-two drop ) 452 72 375 DS
(present with native threads on Solaris!) 154 72 364 DS
(The Tcl byte-code compiler usually, but not always, improves performance. In particular, tests involving I/O tend ) 463 72 343 DS
(to run slower in the byte-code interpreter. Jacl, not surprisingly, is always slowest. We expected this, but were ) 450 72 332 DS
(unprepared for the degree in difference for some of the tests \(two orders of magnitude in some cases\).) 405 72 320 DS
(To be fair, the Jacl release we tested was the first release, and Jacl is a much newer program than Tcl. To get a ) 445 72 299 DS
(better handle on a real life Jacl application, we ran both Jacl and Tcl Blend on the Jacl test suite -- in this context, ) 467 72 288 DS
(Jacl was 6 to 7 times slower than Tcl Blend. There were, however, a number of discrepancies in the test results, ) 452 72 276 DS
(most common being that Tcl Blend failed some tests because of minor differences in return values. We noted that ) 460 72 264 DS
(the test suite calls a lot of Java code, where Jacl and Tcl Blend will run at similar speeds -- it could be that the ) 454 72 253 DS
(test suite is not executing much Tcl code, hiding some of Jacl's poor interpretation performance.) 388 72 241 DS
(Still, the Jacl test suite results are heartening, since it seems that overall Jacl is not as slow as some of the timing ) 461 72 220 DS
(tests indicate. Even so, its overall performance is poor enough that we can only consider it to be an interesting ) 447 72 209 DS
(experiment at the moment.) 108 72 197 DS
(The JavaScript tests are unlike the other tests, since JavaScript runs inside a Web browser. The JavaScript times are ) 467 72 177 DS
(probably higher than they would be if it were possible to run them as a stand-alone program. Comparisons of the ) 461 72 165 DS
(JavaScript times to the other times should be taken with a very large grain of salt. It would be interesting to ) 438 72 153 DS
(compare performance of JavaScript and Java running in the same browser -- some preliminary measurements ) 443 72 141 DS
(indicate that Java code running in a browser runs at about half the speed as when stand-alone.) 378 72 130 DS
(In summary, combining scripting languages with Java offers new opportunities for software development, but with ) 465 72 109 DS
(some interesting and non-obvious performance implications.) 246 72 98 DS
